        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Identify the common multiples of $7$ and $9$ from your lists.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$63$",
          "worked": "The only number appearing in both lists is $63$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "What is the lowest common multiple of $7$ and $9$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$63$",
          "worked": "The smallest number shared by the two lists is $63$, so the LCM is $63$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q5-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "What do you notice? Why does this happen?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The LCM is $7\\times9=63$. Their only common factor is $1$, so they are coprime and their LCM is their product.",
          "worked": "The LCM equals the product $7\\times9=63$. The factors of $7$ are $1,7$; the factors of $9$ are $1,3,9$. Their HCF is $1$, so they are coprime. Using the taught rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{LCM}(7,9)&=\\frac{7\\times9}{1}\\\\&=63.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
